"""THE LINE-F CALL TABLE OF OURS (`src/aes/lineftable.S`, generated by `bench/linef_table.py`) — band 6 wave 0, slice T.

LINKED, NOT BOUND. Both blobs link the table and its thunks; no word of either names them, and the handler gem_entry
copies still loads the ROM's table (`linef_table.PENDING_BINDING`: gemstart.S's eighth relocation, slice B's). So
EVERY RUN HERE THAT REACHES THE TABLE DOES SO THROUGH A LABELLED STAGING — the one operand laid by the harness, in
this module's own machines, and said in the test's name: `$cc24` of a ROM-booted machine (`ThroughTheTable`), or the
operand of the blob's own handler before the blob is laid for an our-boot (`staged_for_an_our_boot`).

WHAT IS HELD.
  * THE FILE is the generator's text; the kind is its own of the build contract.
  * THE 658 ENTRIES, entry by entry and both ways, the verdict of each DERIVED HERE from the ROM's table bytes and
    the build's own symbols — not read from the generator: ours where the build has an entry for the ROM's routine (a
    byte-pinned `.S` entry; a C core with a declared Alcyon signature, through its thunk), the ROM's own longword in
    the rest, none dead.
  * EVERY THUNK IS ITS ROUTINE'S SIGNATURE, read off the blob's bytes: each argument's place in the caller's frame,
    its width, its extension, the order of the pushes, the host argument, the image, the core it calls, the frame it
    drops. (A missing sign extension is held HERE and nowhere else: GCC reads a word argument's low word alone, so no
    run can show one.)
  * EVERY THUNK A CASE CAN ENTER IS ENTERED: the routine's own registered case, run on the blob THROUGH THE TABLE —
    the ROM's own call word, the ROM's handler in RAM, our table, our thunk, our core — against the ROM's run of the
    same case through the same word; WHO RAN read off the cycle profile (the ROM routine never entered, the thunk and
    the core entered, the thunk's cycles its instructions' own). A thunk no case can enter is UNENTERED BY NAME
    (`UNENTERED`), exact both ways.
  * THE CORES THAT TAKE THE CALLER'S RETURN SITE are handed the real one (the word after the call word).
  * THE STAGED OUR-BOOT OVER THE TABLE reaches the desk's first idle — ours — with the ROM's own screen, byte for byte.

WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT ENTER (slice H's finding): the kit's 68000 executes `$F620..$F627` as MOVE16 instead of
taking the Line-F exception. The two entries those call words name are held by the table's bytes and listed
(`UNENTERED_BY_THE_ORACLE`); no entry of ours is called by such a word.
"""
